{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1784848608,"nonce":"bioOLSnupg0cCxm6d5WlWzjsdW0=","message":"## Design note: a unified fix for all three gaps (analysis, not yet built)\n\nAsked to check whether one approach fixes gaps 1-3 together rather than\npiecemeal. It does — the three gaps are one root cause with three faces.\n\n### Root cause: `has_newer` is not modelled as first-class\n\n'Is a newer version available?' is an axis orthogonal to `status`\n(reviewing/waiting/…). It is computed once in _load_series\n(_tracking_app.py:1030-1032, `newest_recorded \u003e current_rev`) and then\neach of three consumers mishandles it:\n\n- **Display (gap 2):** only _show_details reads it (2045-2063).\n  SeriesRow.compose (540-619), both list sorts (1077-1085), and the\n  `s:`/`t:` limit filter (1105-1129) are all blind to it — hence no row\n  marker, date-order sort, and no 'list of upgradable series'.\n- **Actions (gap 1):** the upgrade action gates on `status`\n  (_STATE_ACTIONS/_GATED_ACTIONS 1249-1327; action_update_revision floor\n  at 3781), which excludes `waiting` — so upgrade is unreachable without a\n  Review round-trip.\n- **Bulk update (gap 3):** update_series_tracking tries to fold has_newer\n  into `status` via auto-promotion — the part that was unreliable.\n  Fixed on feature/waiting-promote-watermark (schema v11 watermark).\n\nFixing these separately risks visible inconsistency (a row marker that\nsays 'upgrade available' while auto-promote won't fire but manual upgrade\nwould); they only reconcile if designed as a set.\n\n### Unified approach: surface has_newer as a first-class attribute\n\nDrive all three consumers from the single existing flag:\n1. Display: SeriesRow renders an upgrade glyph; add a has_newer sort key;\n   add an `up:` limit token for the upgradable list. (gap 2)\n2. Actions: gate upgrade on has_newer, not status; invoking it on a\n   `waiting` series does promote+switch-revision in one step — Mark's\n   'upgrade and return to reviewing in one step'. (gap 1)\n3. Bulk update: auto-promotion stays, consistent with the visible signal.\n   (gap 3)\n\n### The one product fork\n\n- **(A) Keep auto-promotion:** [u]pdate still auto-upgrades waiting\n  series; the v11 watermark stays as the correctness base; gaps 1+2 layer\n  on as one 'surface has_newer' change. Lowest risk.\n- **(B) Drop auto-promotion:** status never auto-mutates; has_newer is\n  purely orthogonal, actioned on demand. This *dissolves* gap 3 (no\n  auto-mutation → no re-wake loop → the watermark becomes redundant and\n  would be reverted), giving a simpler state machine — but loses the bulk\n  convenience and contradicts the 'will auto-upgrade on update' hint\n  (_tracking_app.py:2052), so it is a product decision.\n\nBoth fix all three from one design; they differ only on whether [u]pdate\nshould mutate state as a side effect. Decision deferred; gap 3 fix stays\nlanded either way (revertible under B).","files":null}]}